# SU(3) small-ball coefficient c = lim V(s)/s^4

Method: Weyl formula, small-s limit: |Delta|^2 -> prod(th_j-th_k)^2, f -> sum th^2/6, so region is disc r<=sqrt(6s) in plane sum th=0.
Polar coords th_j=sqrt(2/3) r cos(phi+2 pi j/3); dth1 dth2 = r dr dphi/sqrt3 (numeric Jacobian at r=1: 0.577350, 1/sqrt3=0.577350).
Angular integral of prod^2 (midpoint, N=4000, spectrally exact): 1.57079633 (pi/2=1.57079633).
Radial: int r^7 dr via Gauss-Legendre = 0.12500000 (1/8).

Deterministic quadrature: c = 0.620245
Closed form: Delta^2 = r^6 sin^2(3 phi)/2 (cubic discriminant), so
c = (1/6)(4 pi^2)^-1 (1/sqrt3) (pi/2)(1/8) 6^4 = 9 sqrt3/(8 pi) = 27/(8 sqrt3 pi) = 0.620245
Agrees with prior MC ~0.620. No failures. (Limit only; no finite-s correction check done.)

